eserve\nthe core `Store` lifecycle, notify Coaction after direct external immutable writes, and declare\nsupport boundaries with contract tests. See contributing before\nstarting one."],[255,"Custom external runtime"],[715,"Data-only representation"],[169,"Declare a container for dynamic data:"],[141,"Declare mode for ambiguous objects"],[608,"Dedicated Worker"],[137,"Deep tracking is suited to sparse reads. For full collection scans, keep a native getter over a\nfrozen snapshot; `derive(store, s => s.total)` can compose that getter. Reading every item through\na deep proxy adds tracking work without avoiding any evaluation. Immutable updates still copy\nchanged ancestor containers, so a flat-array scalar write is not O(depth) independent of size.\n`pnpm benchmark:derived` reports sparse workloads at 1k/10k/50k scales, full scans at 10k,\ncached reads, and writes with unread/disposed derivations. It is included in `benchmark:check`."],[814,"Default synchronous authority"],[509,"Defaults to `localStorage`. Pass a `storage`, or\n`@coaction/sync/indexeddb` for a larger durable store:"],[618,"Degraded tabs run independent local state. If every tab must see the same state, treat a missing\nSharedWorker as a hard error instead of relying on the fallback. The degraded store has\n`share === false` and owns its state locally, so its low-level `setState()` and `apply()` methods\nremain available. Always invoke actions through `getState()` and await them before reading their\neffects."],[485,"Delivery"],[554,"dense arrays of supported values;"],[5,"Design rules worth knowing early"],[600,"designing idempotent actions or application-level deduplication;"],[615,"Destroy the Coaction store when the client no longer owns the connection. Terminate the Worker\naccording to your application's ownership model."],[164,"Destroy the store when ownership ends"],[736,"Direct answers about local use, selectors, serialization, schemas, workers, adapters, and DevTools."],[824,"Direct client `setState()`/`apply()`"],[850,"Direct client atom writes rejected"],[375,"Direct client writes"],[838,"Direct client Zustand writes rejected"],[738,"Do I have to use `observer()` in React?"],[295,"Do not assume every patch pair contains leaf paths. Positional edits can fall back to\nreplacement patches at changed top-level keys; local object graphs can need a root snapshot\nat `path: []`. Replay must preserve the committed values and reference topology.\nLocal cycles are supported as complete values during initialization or replacement. Creating\ncycles from draft references and editing within cyclic drafts remain outside the recipe\ncontract. These local guarantees do not extend the shared JSON contract."],[237,"Do not begin by moving the store into a worker. First reproduce the current local behavior:"],[244,"Do not convert solely to preserve the word “slice.” Keep a single store when the flat shape is\nclearer."],[78,"Do not deploy a new shared client against an authority running a different\nCoaction major. Upgrade the Worker/SharedWorker authority and all clients as\none deployment unit."],[663,"Do not imply slices support for a whole-store adapter."],[352,"Do not infer a universal write penalty from one microbenchmark. In the measured 4.0 scenario that\nupdates one item and then reads a derived total across 1,000 items, Coaction reaches about 55% of the\nthroughput of the equivalent Zustand selector case and is far behind a hand-maintained field. A\nseparate bulk benchmark shows that other update shapes can be comparable or favor Mutative. For\nhigh-frequency drag, animation frames, and streaming tick handlers, benchmark the representative\noperation and skip Coaction when it misses the required budget."],[575,"Do not place a function inside a data object and expect it to behave like an action."],[324,"Do not publish one of those cases as a universal “faster than Zustand” claim. Zustand can make a\nderived read constant-time by storing the derived value, but application actions must then keep it\nconsistent. Coaction's proposition is maintained cached derivation, not winning every mutation\nmicrobenchmark."],[748,"Does Coaction have Redux DevTools integration?"],[747,"Does Coaction include signal primitives?"],[595,"duplicate suppression and gap detection;"],[625,"Each authority lifetime owns a unique epoch and a sequence that begins at zero. A client:"],[505,"Each call is given its own copy of everything, so a resolver that works on what\nit is handed cannot disturb the rebase or the next call."],[131,"Each read keeps its current dependencies until `dispose()` or `store.destroy()`. Unread\nderivations do not activate patches. Disposing is idempotent, releases the read's dependencies\nand cache, and makes subsequent calls throw. Stopping a consumer does not dispose a store-owned\nderivation: it stays available for other consumers. Native getter caches have their own existing\nstore lifetime."],[617,"Each tab gets a client mirror while the SharedWorker owns one state instance. Passing the explicit\n`worker` key with an `undefined` value degrades to a local authority. Its `getState()` actions still\nreturn promises and its state, action arguments, and action results obey the shared JSON contract,\nso awaited call sites remain portable between modes."],[687,"Enable forward/inverse patch generation locally"],[685,"Enhancers applied in array order before state materialization"],[603,"ensuring state, arguments, and results obey the JSON contract."],[207,"Entry"],[43,"Entry-point map"],[611,"Epochs and sequence recovery"],[887,"Every authority and connected client must use the same Coaction major and compatible wire\nprotocol. Mixed-major workers, shared workers, and custom transports are unsupported even if the\nTypeScript surface appears compatible."],[543,"Every value crossing shared mode is encoded as JSON. Coaction validates values before they enter\nthe wire protocol rather than relying on `JSON.stringify()` to silently no